    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d348">

                <pb facs="0590" n="576" sameAs="frus:frus1950v06/pg576"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">320/11–1650: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The United States Representative at the United
                        Nations</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Austin</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret   priority</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">New York</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1950-11-16T14:05:00-05:00">November 16,
                            1950—2:05 p. m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    Delga 304. Re postponement consideration of
                    Formosan item. At Pakistan dinner last night considerable number delegates
                    expressed interest in “real reason” behind our motion in First Committee to
                    postpone consideration Formosan item and our views in general as to where do we
                    go from here on complex of Chinese items in Assembly and SC. Ross responded as follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>At beginning of Assembly it seemed probable that Korean war would be
                            successfully liquidated in fairly short time and that UN might then
                            reasonably turn its attention to two constructive programs: (<hi
                                rendition="#italic">a</hi>) unification and rehabilitation of Korea,
                            and (<hi rendition="#italic">b</hi>) initiation at least of
                            consideration of problem of future status of Formosa. Korean Commission
                            was on its way to Korea and would do what it could in light of
                            circumstances in pushing forward towards unification and rehabilitation.
                            On other hand, did not seem wise to us in light of changed military
                            situation in North Korea to plunge into consideration Formosan question.
                            On contrary, seemed wiser to us to play for time pending clarification
                            military situation. Furthermore, pending clarification military
                            situation Korea, it seemed essential maintain protection Formosan
                            flank.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>Chauvel, Berendsen, Entezam and Santa Cruz,<anchor n="1" xml:id="d348-f1"/> with
                    whom conversations held, expressed full agreement course taken.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Austin</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d348-f1" n="1" xml:id="d348-f1-content">
                <p>Jean Chauvel of France, Sir Carl Berendsen of New Zealand, Nasrollah Entezam of
                    Iran, and Hernán Santa Cruz of Chile, Representatives to the Fifth Session of
                    the U.N. General Assembly.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
